            <fr:title text="The Coherence Theorem for Monoidal Categories using Displayed
Category Theory">The Coherence Theorem for Monoidal Categories using Displayed
Category Theory</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The coherence theorem for monoidal categories can be formulated as
saying that the <fr:link href="/sss-0063/" title="Free Monoidal Category over a Set" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0063/" display-uri="sss-0063" type="local">free monoidal category</fr:link> is <fr:link href="/sss-006I/" title="Thin Category" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-006I/" display-uri="sss-006I" type="local">thin</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>We can prove this statement in a few steps. We first invoke the
following two lemmas,</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>26</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/sss-006F.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-006F/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>sss-006F</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/sss-006F/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Retraction Lemma for the Coherence Theorem">Retraction Lemma for the Coherence Theorem</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p> Fix a set <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex>. Given a monodial category <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M]]></fr:tex>, a strong monoidal functor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F : M
\to  \mathsf {FreeMon}{\mathopen {}\left (X\right )\mathclose {}}]]></fr:tex>, and an interpretation
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\iota  : X \rightsquigarrow  \mathsf {IsoFiber_{F}}]]></fr:tex> we can use the
<fr:link href="/sss-0064/" title="Global Elimination Principle for the Free Monoidal Category" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0064/" display-uri="sss-0064" type="local">global elimination principle of the free monoidal category</fr:link>
to construct a global section of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {IsoFiber_{F}}]]></fr:tex>. Then by using the
<fr:link href="/sss-006G/" title="Universal Property of IsoFiber" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-006G/" display-uri="sss-006G" type="local">universal property of IsoFiber</fr:link>, we can extract the
construction of a functor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G : \mathsf {FreeMon}{\mathopen {}\left (X\right )\mathclose {}} \to  M]]></fr:tex> such that
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F \circ  G \cong  \mathsf {Id}]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>26</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/sss-006H.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-006H/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>sss-006H</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/sss-006H/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Thin IsoRetract">Thin IsoRetract</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D]]></fr:tex> is <fr:link href="/sss-006I/" title="Thin Category" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-006I/" display-uri="sss-006I" type="local">thin</fr:link> and there are functors <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F : C \to  D]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G : D
\to  C]]></fr:tex> such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G \circ  F \cong  \mathsf {Id}]]></fr:tex>, then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> is also thin.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>That is, we reduce the problem to finding a thin monoidal category <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M]]></fr:tex> with
a strong monoidal functor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F : M
\to  \mathsf {FreeMon}{\mathopen {}\left (X\right )\mathclose {}}]]></fr:tex>, and an interpretation
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\iota  : X \rightsquigarrow  \mathsf {IsoFiber_{F}}]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>From here, we can construct such a category by using a discrete
category of lists over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex>. This category is discrete (the only
morphisms are identities), so it is trivally thin. Further, this
category of lists can be shown to have a weak initiality property that
gives a strong monoidal functor into any monoidal category
interpreting <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex>. In particular, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FreeMon}{\mathopen {}\left (X\right )\mathclose {}}]]></fr:tex> interprets <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> so we
get the desired strong monoidal functor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>. From here we can also
construct such an <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\iota ]]></fr:tex> and complete the proof, but this is
somewhat uninspired without going further into details.
</html:p>
            <html:p>The bulk of the proof is really in showing that this category of
lists has the weak initiality property, so I should write that up as well.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
